WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.100
Hey Wisecrack, Jared as always.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:09.790
So Ubisoft recently reached out to us about doing something for the newly remastered version of

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:12.240
Assassin's Creed Rogue for PS4 and Xbox One.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:17.680
Lucky for them, we’ve gotten a lot of AC requests over the years, and lucky for us,

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:22.380
Rogue has a unique perspective on the eternal struggle between the Assassins and Templars,

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:25.630
and how it relates to the complicated nature of freedom.

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:28.731
Throughout the series the value of freedom is placed above all else.

00:00:28.731 --> 00:00:31.210
"How else will we ensure freedom for the human race?"

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:34.910
The assassins, we’re told, are fighting the good fight against those devious Templars

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:37.810
who are trying to rule the world with an iron fist.

00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:43.040
While freedom is nearly universally celebrated in society and media, cue the legally safe

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:49.410
William Wallace knock-off, "FREEEEDOM!" through its protagonist Shay Cormac, Assassin’s

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:55.180
Creed: Rogue asks an interesting and unique question: when does freedom go too far?

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:57.940
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on Assassin’s Creed: Rogue Remastered.

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:00.800
And, as always, some minor spoilers ahead.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:02.030
First a quick recap.

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:06.939
Rogue takes place around the numerically challenged Seven Years’ War and somewhere between Black

00:01:06.939 --> 00:01:08.940
Flag and Assassin’s Creed 3.

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:13.330
We follow fledgling Assassin Shay Cormac, a trouble maker who begins to question the

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:15.050
Assassin Brotherhood’s methods.

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:21.820
"Laurence Washington is dead" "You look disappointed"
"The sickly way that man looked.

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:25.100
He'd have been dead in a month anyway."

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:29.420
He continues to struggle with defending his creed until halfway through the game, he is

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:33.920
given an assignment to find a Piece of Eden an ancient object said to bestow God like

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:34.979
power and wisdom.

00:01:34.979 --> 00:01:39.409
"Be careful Shay, Pieces of Eden are powerful relics."

00:01:39.409 --> 00:01:44.500
When Shay tries to retrieve it, he triggers
the infamous Lisbon earthquake of 1755 that

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:45.500
killed thousands.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:48.200
"What's the next city you want me to smite.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:52.909
What happened in Haiti happened in Portugal a great earthquake!

00:01:52.909 --> 00:01:53.909
Thousands dead!"

00:01:53.909 --> 00:01:57.340
He confronts the Assassins who deny culpability for the earthquake, even though they were

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:02.150
aware that the same thing had caused the devastating earthquake in Port-au-Prince five years earlier.

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:06.680
Shay argues that, “Who are you to decide what city falls next,” but the Assassins

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:08.940
say, “We have the responsibility!”

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:10.920
So Shay flees the Brotherhood.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:14.799
After being injured in his escape he is nursed back to health by an elderly couple who turn

00:02:14.799 --> 00:02:16.360
out to be Templars.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:20.959
Shay is then recruited by the Templars, and sent to kill his former Assassin bretheren.

00:02:20.959 --> 00:02:25.120
In most Assassin’s Creed games we’re told the Templars are evil because they oppose

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:29.370
freedom, and the Assassins are good because they fight for freedom, which is best embodied

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:33.170
in their mantra: "Nothing is True, Everything is permitted."

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:38.650
But AC Rogue, by showing the inner workings of both sides, explores the paradoxical nature

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:42.120
of freedom, and the shortcomings of the Assassin’s belief system.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:46.879
Sure, the Assassins seem noble; after all, you don their mantle as you fight against

00:02:46.879 --> 00:02:49.919
the corruption of the church and history’s other bad-guys.

00:02:49.919 --> 00:02:53.790
But just how benign is the saying “Nothing is true.

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:54.950
Everything is permitted.”

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:59.200
When you consider the events of Rogue, the Assassins’ disposition toward absolute freedom

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:01.209
starts to look a bit questionable.

00:03:01.209 --> 00:03:05.340
We can best understand why through the concept of positive freedom.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:10.320
Positive freedom is the freedom to do something, and to self-determine what that something is.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:15.120
It’s the freedom to choose to blast weird
German techno whenever you want That’s different

00:03:15.129 --> 00:03:20.099
than negative freedom - the freedom from certain external forces, like the freedom from your

00:03:20.099 --> 00:03:24.640
roommate Brad telling you to stop blasting weird German techno at 2 AM.

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:26.410
Or Brad commandeering your stereo.

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:30.920
"The problem with unlimited freedom is that it often comes in conflict with the rights

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:32.609
and freedoms of others.

00:03:32.609 --> 00:03:37.180
While you may be the master of your own fate, what if your actions limit the freedom of others?

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:41.380
What if Brad can’t be the master of his
own destiny because he hasn’t gotten any

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:46.209
sleep after the unceasing onslaught of Autobahn every night until 2AM?

00:03:46.209 --> 00:03:50.829
And, if you reject any limitations on the
freedom from outside interference, or negative

00:03:50.829 --> 00:03:54.029
freedom, who’s going to step in to help Brad get some sleep?"

00:03:54.029 --> 00:03:58.469
If “Everything is permitted” then people might not agree with when and how I want to

00:03:58.469 --> 00:03:59.549
listen to music.

00:03:59.549 --> 00:04:03.229
The Templars address this problem of freedom by negating it altogether.

00:04:03.229 --> 00:04:08.329
They, in essence, don’t believe in the value of positive freedom, that people can make

00:04:08.329 --> 00:04:13.209
their own decisions, or negative freedom, that they should be free from despotic control.

00:04:13.209 --> 00:04:17.341
Their philosophy is, essentially, that the freedom of one person will ultimately end

00:04:17.341 --> 00:04:22.540
up rubbing against someone else’s, and so such freedom must be restricted.

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:26.880
Philosopher Isiah Berlin highlights one way in which the self-determination of positive

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:29.170
freedom can have horrific results.

00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:33.140
Freedom, for Berlin, can often be defined collectively with others.

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:37.750
Groups, tribes, or even nations can set out to define their own destiny.

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:42.090
And that’s where things get a little tricky, since in the name of a collective freedom

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:47.000
many have decided to repress minority groups - the German people collectively yearned for

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:52.000
the freedom to determine their national destiny, but, well, we all know how that turned out

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:53.840
for certain groups in Europe.

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:58.620
Obviously the assassins and the nazis are about as similar as Motzart and Fred Durst,

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:02.970
but the assassins are willing to sacrifice the freedom of some for the sake of freedom

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:04.400
for everybody.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:08.780
If we are all free to do whatever the hell we want, and the Assassins don’t mind earthquakes

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:13.250
killing thousands, then is absolute Freedom really the ultimate good?

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:17.630
The Templars seem to understand this problem with positive freedom, with people being their

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:22.190
own masters, so they actively try to suppress it in the name of a better world.

00:05:22.190 --> 00:05:27.140
"What marvelous destruction captain, I saw the smoke all the way from the Morrigan"

00:05:27.140 --> 00:05:29.520
"That should discourage those miscreants.

00:05:29.530 --> 00:05:35.470
New York is safe for now," And, as Rogue shows, they’re not as misguided as one may think.

00:05:35.470 --> 00:05:40.130
Paradoxically, certain rights and freedoms can be enhanced with a heavy hand from say,

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:41.910
the government or the Templars.

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:47.170
So for instance, by limiting someone’s freedom to enslave another person, law can instate

00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:51.530
a better version of freedom not predicated on owning other humans.

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:56.730
We see this conundrum in other AC games, such as Black Flag where the British templars crack

00:05:56.730 --> 00:06:02.160
down on the Pirates you play as in the name of safer seas, and liberty for would-be pirate victims.

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:07.000
The Templars believe they know best and that order can be achieved through guidance and

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:10.220
rule, but this isn’t exactly a perfect ideology either.

00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:14.570
The total control of individuals for the sake of the greater good leads to your typical

00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:16.760
Big-Brother dystopian nightmare.

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:21.950
So while causing earthquakes, stealing fortunes and slitting throats is okay if "Nothing is

00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:26.500
True, Everything is permitted," the polar opposite is that we should all be thanking

00:06:26.500 --> 00:06:29.170
our masters for living under a jackboot.

00:06:29.170 --> 00:06:31.010
But hey, at least they’re open about it.

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:36.010
What makes Rogue unique is that Shay distances himself from the dogmatic belief structures

00:06:36.010 --> 00:06:41.820
that characterize the protagonists of previous games and embraces a rationally informed moral choice.

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:43.940
But does Shay make the right choice?

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:47.870
To figure that out, we’ll need help from one of the big dawgs of the European Enlightenment-

00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:49.060
Immanuel Kant.

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:53.690
During the Enlightenment, Kant formulated a way to ground morality in reason instead

00:06:53.690 --> 00:06:57.670
of religion or custom known as the ‘Categorical Imperative’.

00:06:57.670 --> 00:07:00.060
So let’s see how Shay’s decision stacks up.

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:04.620
"The first formulation: “Act only according to that maxim whereby you can... will that

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:08.750
it should become a universal law,” sort of boils down to ‘Do Unto Others’.

00:07:08.750 --> 00:07:13.530
So, if you want to know if killing people and then searching their bodies for loot is

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:17.440
moral, you should consider what will happen if everyone follows this rule.

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:21.730
The second is to “Act in such a way that you treat humanity... never merely as a means

00:07:21.730 --> 00:07:25.230
to an end, but always at the same time as an end.”

00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:30.480
This essentially means, life is the highest value in and of itself, so never harm others

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:34.010
or exploit them as a mean to achieve some “bigger” goal.

00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:38.040
The final rule says that “the will of every rational being” should be thought of “as

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:40.230
a universally legislating will.”

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:45.320
Which ties the first two together and suggests that there is a universal reason or logic

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:48.410
that we can all access through our own rationality.

00:07:48.410 --> 00:07:53.070
It also offers a framework that includes a sense of freedom - since every person is capable

00:07:53.070 --> 00:07:57.660
of participating in this kind of moral reasoning - without some of the dangers of the Templars

00:07:57.660 --> 00:08:04.270
or Assassins, where people become freedomless pawns to their grand plans, even if, paradoxically,

00:08:04.270 --> 00:08:06.410
freedom is part of that grand plan.

00:08:06.410 --> 00:08:11.600
According to Kant, no action or morals are ethical if they contradict these three rules,

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:14.350
so let’s see how Shay scores against the Assassins.

00:08:14.350 --> 00:08:18.870
Shay defects because he believes that by causing an Earthquake and justifying it as part of

00:08:18.870 --> 00:08:23.060
their ‘responsibility’, the Assassins have done a grave injustice.

00:08:23.060 --> 00:08:28.350
This act violates the first formulation because it implies killing thousands for your own

00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:31.850
ends, which could never be a universal ethical act.

00:08:31.850 --> 00:08:34.430
One point for Shay. zero for the Assassins.

00:08:34.430 --> 00:08:39.009
The Assassins are also saying those lives are acceptable sacrifices for their greater

00:08:39.009 --> 00:08:45.380
goal, a violation of the 2nd principle, because using people as a means to an end is a big no-no.

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:47.020
Another point for Shay.

00:08:47.029 --> 00:08:52.149
And finally, through their belief in total Freedom, the Assassins are trampling on people’s

00:08:52.149 --> 00:08:57.579
legislative will by ignoring their own agency and literally destroying their homes and autonomous

00:08:57.579 --> 00:09:00.470
lives, which contradicts Kant’s third formulation.

00:09:00.470 --> 00:09:02.720
Shay wants no part of it.

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:04.790
So 3 points for Shay.

00:09:04.790 --> 00:09:06.290
Zero for the Assassins.

00:09:06.290 --> 00:09:09.019
So what can we learn from Shay’s decision?

00:09:09.019 --> 00:09:11.959
That fighting for freedom is not so simple.

00:09:11.959 --> 00:09:16.300
By pursuing their own freedom, the assassins have destroyed the freedoms of others.

00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:21.050
But by rejecting the indoctrinated philosophy of "nothing is true.

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:26.460
everything is permitted," Shay could be the
few ethical heroes of the Assassin’s Creed franchise.

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:31.360
Unfortunately for Shay, the Templars aren’t
exactly model citizens either.

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:36.720
In the name of regulating freedom for the greater good, the Templars do plenty of deplorable things.

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:40.500
We see them helping local people in various places, “Now let’s use some of

00:09:40.510 --> 00:09:44.079
that money for the good of the city,” and stopping the Assassins from committing what

00:09:44.079 --> 00:09:45.440
amounts to a war crime.

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:49.040
“There is an abandoned factory nearby holding a dangerous poison.”

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:53.389
We also see them lie, maim and kill in the name of their own creed.

00:09:53.389 --> 00:09:58.740
In the end, the same sense of moral dubiousness follows the Templars as much as the Assassins,

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:04.569
"When I'm issued new recruits burdened with regerets, I tell them the surest way to lose

00:10:04.569 --> 00:10:10.569
them is with gunpowder," with them both contradicting Kant’s Imperative by using people as a means

00:10:10.569 --> 00:10:14.149
to their own ends and killing those who stand in their way.

00:10:14.149 --> 00:10:20.600
AC Rogue’s more nuanced exploration of freedom, good, and evil is a welcome addition to the series.

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:25.240
Shay’s decision to betray the Assassins brings the philosophies of both factions under

00:10:25.249 --> 00:10:29.329
scrutiny and makes the player view each in an entirely new light.

00:10:29.329 --> 00:10:32.399
Was this moral ambivalence about freedom always there?

00:10:32.399 --> 00:10:34.449
Have we been playing as the ‘Bad Guys’ all along?

00:10:34.449 --> 00:10:38.980
Here’s to hoping this complex question gets explored in future installments.

00:10:38.980 --> 00:10:43.160
But until then, be sure to check out the remastered version of Assassin’s Creed Rogue out now

00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:45.180
on Xbox One and Ps4.

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:48.480
Be sure to check out the link in the description to learn more about the game!

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:52.060
And you might be interested in checking out this really awesome trailer of the game.

00:10:52.060 --> 00:10:54.600
Thanks again to Ubisoft for giving us this opportunity!

00:10:54.740 --> 00:10:57.240
And as always, thanks for watching guys! Peace!

